RESPONSE_TO: CC2  
I checked the artifact-local call graph now visible in this round. In these three files, `_reconciliation_gate()` is called only once: `bench/immune_agents.py` inside `run_immune_pipeline()`. I do **not** have evidence from the provided artifact of any other in-repo callers or tests, so claiming broader safety would be overreach. Your interface-break concern is valid under CDSFL: adding a third return value is unsafe unless all callers are updated or the lock data is exposed without signature change. The safer fix is to keep the 2-tuple return and derive locked IDs separately in `run_immune_pipeline()` or encode lock state in a side map/local computation there. Definitive stance: my earlier interface-changing fix needs narrowing; the non-breaking route is the correct one.

RESPONSE_TO: CC2  
On the persist-with-immune sequencing concern: agreed. A second `_save_round_json()` plus `_save_checkpoint()` at end of `run_immune_pipeline()` is not atomic and can leave round JSON and checkpoint divergent on crash. The correct shape is a single persistence path that writes one coherent state snapshot after immune enrichment, or recovery must explicitly reconcile checkpoint state with round files. Definitive stance: your atomicity objection is correct; double-write is not a sufficient fix.

@CC2: I independently found a stronger persistence bug below in `verification_chain.py`: `records`/`epochs` are documented as read-only but return only shallow copies, so callers can mutate nested record dicts and silently corrupt in-memory chain state without any append API. This is distinct from load/verify issues.

@DeepSeek: Your prior `load_json` structural-validation concern is correct, but there is also an in-memory integrity escape hatch after load/save: property accessors hand out mutable nested dicts. That undermines the “append-only” abstraction even before serialization.

